      body: 'Abuela Rosa kept an old light switch on the shelf above her stove in a village in the hills of Oaxaca, Mexico. It was not connected to anything. It was cracked, painted green, and screwed to a small board. Her grandchildren, Mateo and Lucía, visited from the city every summer, and every summer they complained that the internet in the village was too slow to watch anything. One evening Lucía finally pointed at the shelf and asked what the switch was for. Abuela Rosa sat down and told them. When she was nine, there was no power in the village at all. Her mother cooked by firelight and the children did their lessons before the sun went down. Then one spring the wires came up the mountain, and the whole village stood in the plaza while the priest prayed and the first switch was turned. The lights came on, and an old man began to cry. That was the switch. She had kept it for fifty years so that somebody would ask. Mateo looked at his phone, then at the switch, and laughed at himself. That night the three of them wrote a list on the back of a calendar of everything in the house that would have been a wonder in Abuela’s childhood. The list ran out of room. Abuela read them a verse before bed: "That this may be a sign among you, that when your children ask their fathers in time to come, saying, What mean ye by these stones?" (Joshua 4:6) Lucía said the switch was their stone. The next summer the children brought a stone of their own.',
